The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
list_for_each_entry_safe(smb_lock, tmp_lock, &fp->lock_list, flist) {
    spin_lock(&fp->conn->llist_lock);
    list_del(&smb_lock->clist);
    spin_unlock(&fp->conn->llist_lock);
    // ... (rest of the loop)
}
```
In `__ksmbd_close_fd()`, when `fp->conn` was set to `NULL` by `session_fd_check()`, the loop still attempts to dereference `fp->conn` to access `llist_lock`, leading to use-after-free of the already freed connection object.

Reason:
During session disconnect without logout, `session_fd_check()` sets `fp->conn = NULL` to preserve the durable handle but did not remove byte-range locks (`smb_lock->clist`) from the old connection's `lock_list`. Later, when the durable scavenger calls `__ksmbd_close_fd(NULL, fp)`, the lock cleanup loop tries to `spin_lock(&fp->conn->llist_lock)` on a NULL pointer, accessing freed memory because the connection had already been freed by `ksmbd_tcp_disconnect()`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
1. In `__ksmbd_close_fd()`:
```c
list_for_each_entry_safe(smb_lock, tmp_lock, &fp->lock_list, flist) {
    if (!list_empty(&smb_lock->clist) && fp->conn) {
        spin_lock(&fp->conn->llist_lock);
        list_del(&smb_lock->clist);
        spin_unlock(&fp->conn->llist_lock);
    }
    list_del(&smb_lock->flist);
    locks_free_lock(smb_lock->fl);
    kfree(smb_lock);
}
```
2. In `session_fd_check()`:
```c
list_for_each_entry_safe(smb_lock, tmp_lock, &fp->lock_list, flist) {
    spin_lock(&fp->conn->llist_lock);
    list_del_init(&smb_lock->clist);
    spin_unlock(&fp->conn->llist_lock);
}
fp->conn = NULL;
```
3. In `ksmbd_reopen_durable_fd()`:
```c
fp->conn = conn;
fp->tcon = work->tcon;
list_for_each_entry(smb_lock, &fp->lock_list, flist) {
    spin_lock(&conn->llist_lock);
    list_add_tail(&smb_lock->clist, &conn->lock_list);
    spin_unlock(&conn->llist_lock);
}
```

Reason:
- The fix in `__ksmbd_close_fd()` safely skips `clist` deletion when the list is empty or `fp->conn` is NULL, preventing use-after-free.
- In `session_fd_check()`, locks are removed from the old connection's `lock_list` before nulling `fp->conn`, ensuring no dangling pointers.
- In `ksmbd_reopen_durable_fd()`, locks are re-added to the new connection's `lock_list` after assigning `fp->conn`, maintaining correct lock association across reconnection. This properly handles the asymmetric cleanup across all three paths.

